<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Valentinian I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">321–375</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Valentinian the Great</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Valentinian I was Roman emperor from 364 to 375. A Pannonian officer acclaimed by the army after Jovian's death, he gave the East to his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valens">Valens</a> and ruled the West himself, spending most of his reign fortifying the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> frontiers against the Alemanni, Quadi and Sarmatians. He died of a stroke at Brigetio in 375 while angrily addressing envoys of the Quadi.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/valentinian-i.jpg" alt="Valentinian I" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ANS · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gold_Solidus_of_Valens,_Antioch.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
